{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Tripoli a noble family that ruled the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Levant. He was the son of Pons of Tripoli and Hodierna of Toron and succeeded his father as count in 1152.\n\n* **Family and Succession** * \nRaymond III was married to Eschiva of Bartamon and had at least four children with her. He was succeeded by his son Bohemond IV of Antioch who would go on to become the Prince of Antioch. Raymond III's family was a prominent one in the Crusader states of the Levant and he was a key figure in the politics of the region.\n\n* **Marriage and Children** *\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bartamon and had four children with her. Their children were Bohemond IV of Antioch who would go on to become the Prince of Antioch Raymond of Antioch and Melisende of Tripoli. Raymond III's marriage to Eschiva of Bartamon was an important one in the history of the County of Tripoli as it helped to secure the county's position in the region.\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli played a key role in the politics of the Crusader states in the Levant. He was a member of the Council of Nablus in 1120 and was involved in the politics of the region throughout his life. He was also a key figure in the defense of the County of Tripoli against the Muslim forces of the Ayyubid dynasty.\n\n* **Military Campaigns and Succession Crisis** *\nRaymond III was involved in several military campaigns during his time as count. He played a key role in the defense of the County of Tripoli against the Muslim forces of the Ayyubid dynasty and was also involved in the politics of the region. He was also involved in a succession crisis in the County of Tripoli which saw him at odds with his brother Bertrand of Tripoli. Raymond III ultimately emerged victorious and succeeded his brother as count.\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli d